// A module written the awkward ways, so MacaDoc is read on more than std.
// Every shape here is one that produced wrong output at some point, and this
// blurb is itself one of them: an indented run in a module blurb is an example,
// and it has to arrive as code coloured by the same scanner a signature goes
// through rather than as a sentence.
//
//     import crates/driver/tests/programs/docfixture

//// A slash rule. Four slashes are punctuation someone drew, not a doc
//// comment, so the helper below it stays out of the reference.
undocumented_helper(x: int) -> int =>
    x + 1

/// A signature whose parameter list wraps. Reading one line and stopping
/// published `wrapped(alpha: int,`: an unbalanced paren, no return type.
wrapped(alpha: int,
        beta: int) -> int =>
    alpha + beta

/// A record written on one line. Looking for a `}` of its own would have run
/// to the end of the file.
Compact = { a: int, b: int }

/// A record written the usual way.
Roomy = {
    first:  str
    second: str
}

/// A sum type.
Choice = Yes | No

/// A constant.
Answer = 42

/// Arithmetic is not emphasis: a * b is a product, and 2 * 3 * 4 is another.
///
/// A backtick that never closes is not a code span either: use `foo for that.
/// Delimiters pair left to right within a paragraph, as they do in Markdown,
/// so this one is on its own: a stray backtick and a real pair in the same
/// paragraph is genuinely ambiguous and nothing can tell them apart.
///
/// But *this* is emphasis and `this` is code.
punctuated(x: int) -> int =>
    x

/// A doc comment can carry markup (<b>, &amp; and </b>), and none of it may
/// reach the page as markup.
metacharacters(x: int) -> int =>
    x

/// A signature that names two documented types, which is what makes a
/// cross-link. std documents no record and no sum, so without this the
/// reference is generated over a corpus where the linking never runs.
widen(r: Roomy) -> Compact =>
    Compact { a = r.first.length(), b = r.second.length() }

/// A doc comment that carries examples, which are code and are coloured as
/// code.
///
/// An indented run carries no tag, so it is read as the language of the file:
///
///     n = widen(Roomy { first = "a", second = "bc" })
///
/// A fence says which language it is:
///
/// ```sh
/// for f in *.maca; do maca run "$f"; done
/// ```
///
/// And a tag no scanner claims comes back as plain text rather than coloured
/// by rules written for something else:
///
/// ```nomad
/// nothing here is coloured: a < b, and it says so.
/// ```
exampled(x: int) -> int =>
    x
